    {"step": 3, "tactic": "DECISIVE — Subject string '/CN=Microsoft Corporation/...' on a self-signed cert added to Root store", "technique": "T1553.004", "what_happened": "Cross-correlation: AMSI 4104 'New-SelfSignedCertificate -Subject \"CN=Microsoft Corporation...\"' (Step 1) + Sysmon EID 13 to HKCU\\...\\Root\\Certificates with cert thumbprint matching the New-SelfSignedCertificate output (Step 2). The Subject impersonates Microsoft but the certificate is NOT issued by Microsoft Root Authority. Rule 91500 triggered.", "chain_reason": "DECISIVE: legitimate corp PKI deployment uses Intune/SCCM signed agents pushing baseline Microsoft Root CAs. The combination (PowerShell New-SelfSignedCertificate + Microsoft Subject impersonation + add to HKCU Root) is mathematically impossible in any legitimate Microsoft PKI workflow."},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Parent chain interactive (explorer.exe → powershell.exe)", "technique": "T1553.004", "what_happened": "Sysmon EID 1: powershell.exe ParentImage=explorer.exe (interactive user session). NO signed corp Intune/Configmgr agent involved.", "chain_reason": "Interactive user driving cert manipulation = malicious operator pattern."}
